From: Khovanskiy <a@khovanskiy.ru>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] driver: keep the symbols of the connector inside the
 driver

The connector is linked into the driver as a static library together
with a zlib of its own, and the driver exported all their symbols.
libz.so.1 of the system comes into the process with the driver as a
dependency of libcrypto, and a library loaded with the driver looks up
its symbols in the driver before itself. So the inner calls of the
system zlib went to the zlib of the connector: deflateInit2_ of one
zlib called deflateReset of the other, and once the driver was loaded,
a gzip stream of any module of the process ran on two zlibs at once.

With versions apart it crashes the process. MariaDB Connector/C 3.0.2
has zlib 1.2.5 and Debian 13 has 1.3.1: after require('mysql'),
deflateInit2_ through ffi.load('z') fell with SIGSEGV in deflateReset.
The zlib of Connector/C 3.4.10 is 1.3.1 too, so on Debian 13 the two
agree by chance. Where libcrypto is not linked with zlib, as on Ubuntu
22.04, the system zlib is loaded apart from the driver and keeps its
calls; where it is and the versions differ, the process crashes.

The driver is linked with --exclude-libs,ALL: the symbols of the static
libraries stay inside it, and it exports the Lua entry point and the
other functions of driver.c only. The connector calls its own zlib, and
the system zlib calls itself. The macOS linker has no such option, and
a library there calls its own functions directly anyway.
---
 mysql/CMakeLists.txt | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/mysql/CMakeLists.txt b/mysql/CMakeLists.txt
index 748498f..85484e5 100644
--- a/mysql/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/mysql/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -2,6 +2,17 @@ add_library(driver SHARED driver.c)
 add_dependencies(driver mariadbclient)
 target_link_libraries(driver mariadbclient)
 
+# The connector comes with a zlib of its own. A library loaded with the
+# driver, as libz of the system pulled in by libcrypto, looks up its
+# symbols in the driver first, so exported, that zlib would take over
+# the inner calls of the system one, and a gzip stream of any module of
+# the process would run on two zlibs at once. The symbols of the static
+# libraries stay inside the driver. The macOS linker has no such option,
+# and a library there calls its own functions directly anyway.
+if (NOT APPLE)
+    target_link_libraries(driver -Wl,--exclude-libs,ALL)
+endif()
+
 # Check 'makecontext', 'getcontext', 'setcontext' and 'swapcontext' symbols.
 
 include(CheckLibraryExists)
-- 
